<p>The department issued benchmark revisions to U.S. factory orders for the years 1987 through April 1997.</p>
<p>As a result of the revisions that include updating seasonal adjustment factors and correcting for late receipts, the department said that total new orders received by U.S. factories for all of 1996 rose 4.6 percent instead of a previously published 4.9 percent increase.</p>
<p>The April inventories-to-shipments ratio was 1.35 months' worth, down from 1.36 months' worth in March. The April ratio was revised from a previously reported 1.37 months' worth and the March ratio from 1.38.</p>
<p>The department reported initial data on April orders for factory orders on June 4.</p>
